    "ex-syg-sign-condition-and-negative-estimate": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-finite-population-and-sampling-design",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $N\\ge1$ be an integer, let $U=\\{1,\\ldots,N\\}$, and let\n$y:U\\to\\mathbb R$ be a fixed function. The pair $(U,y)$ is a **finite\npopulation**; its **total** and **mean** are\n$$T(y):=\\sum_{i\\in U}y_i,\\qquad \\bar y_U:=T(y)/N.$$\n\nA **sampling design** on $U$ is a nonempty finite set\n$\\mathcal S_d\\subseteq\\mathcal P(U)$ and a function\n$p_d:\\mathcal S_d\\to(0,1]$ with $\\sum_{s\\in\\mathcal S_d}p_d(s)=1$.\nHere $\\mathcal S_d$ is the support of the design law: zero-weight subsets are\nomitted. Thus $(\\mathcal S_d,p_d)$ is a finite probability space in the sense\nof [[def-finite-probability-space-and-event]]. For $A\\subseteq\\mathcal S_d$\nand $g:\\mathcal S_d\\to\\mathbb R$, put\n$$P_d(A):=\\sum_{s\\in A}p_d(s),\\qquad E_d[g]:=\\sum_{s\\in\\mathcal S_d}p_d(s)g(s).$$\nThe random sample is the subset $S\\in\\mathcal S_d$; probability and\nexpectation here average over the design while holding every $y_i$ fixed.\n\nFor $i,j\\in U$, define the **inclusion indicator**\n$$I_i(s):=\\begin{cases}1,&i\\in s,\\\\0,&i\\notin s,\\end{cases}$$\nthe **first-order inclusion probability**\n$$\\pi_i:=P_d(\\{s\\in\\mathcal S_d:i\\in s\\}),$$\nand the **second-order inclusion probability**\n$$\\pi_{ij}:=P_d(\\{s\\in\\mathcal S_d:i,j\\in s\\}).$$\nIn particular, $\\pi_{ii}=\\pi_i$.\n\nThe observed record includes the selected unit labels and their values. For a\ncommon record space set\n$$\\mathcal X_d:=\\{(s,z):s\\in\\mathcal S_d,\\ z:s\\to\\mathbb R\\},\\qquad \\mathcal A_d:=\\mathcal P(\\mathcal X_d),$$\nand for each $y\\in\\mathbb R^U$ define\n$$O_y(s):=(s,y|_s),\\qquad P_y^d(B):=\\sum_{\\substack{s\\in\\mathcal S_d\\\\O_y(s)\\in B}}p_d(s)\\quad(B\\in\\mathcal A_d).$$\nFor a real-valued statistic $h$ on $\\mathcal X_d$, write\n$$E_y^d[h]:=\\sum_{s\\in\\mathcal S_d}p_d(s)h(O_y(s))=E_d[h\\circ O_y];$$\nthis is expectation under the induced observed-data law.\nThe local well-definedness result\n[[lem-finite-population-design-law-is-a-probability-measure]] shows that each\n$P_y^d$ is a probability measure on $(\\mathcal X_d,\\mathcal A_d)$. Hence\n$$\\mathsf E_d:=((\\mathcal X_d,\\mathcal A_d),\\mathbb R^U,(P_y^d)_{y\\in\\mathbb R^U})$$\nis a statistical experiment ([[def-statistical-experiment]]), with parametric\nmodel $\\mathsf P_d(y)=P_y^d$ ([[def-parametric-statistical-model]]). The total\n$y\\mapsto T(y)$ and mean $y\\mapsto\\bar y_U$ are estimands\n([[def-estimand-on-a-statistical-model]]).\n\nThis is design probability: the $y_i$ are fixed and only $S$ is random. A\nsuperpopulation model instead assigns a probability law to the values $y_i$;\nthat additional law is separate from $p_d$. The finite-population target and\nthe sampling unit must be recorded in the scientific model contract\n([[def-scientific-statistical-model-contract]]). In fixed-design regression,\nthe matrix $X$ is held fixed and has no selection law; that convention alone\ndoes not specify a sampling design on $U$\n([[def-fixed-design-linear-regression-model]]).",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-inclusion-covariance-difference",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(U,y)$ be a finite population with a sampling design as in\n[[def-finite-population-and-sampling-design]]. For each ordered pair\n$(i,j)\\in U\\times U$, the **inclusion-covariance difference** is\n$$\\Delta_{ij}:=\\pi_{ij}-\\pi_i\\pi_j,$$\nwhere $\\pi_i$ is the probability that unit $i$ is included and $\\pi_{ij}$ is\nthe probability that both $i$ and $j$ are included. The supplier convention\n$\\pi_{ii}=\\pi_i$ gives $\\Delta_{ii}=\\pi_i(1-\\pi_i)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-sen-yates-grundy-variance-identity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(U,y)$ be a finite population with a fixed-size sampling design as in\n[[def-finite-population-and-sampling-design]], so every supported sample has\nthe same size $n$. Assume $\\pi_i>0$ for every $i\\in U$ and put\n$x_i:=y_i/\\pi_i$. Then\n$$\\sum_{j\\in U}\\Delta_{ij}=0\\quad(i\\in U),$$\nand the Horvitz–Thompson variance satisfies the Sen–Yates–Grundy identity\n$$\\operatorname{Var}_d(\\widehat T_{HT})=-\\frac12\\sum_{i,j\\in U}\\Delta_{ij}(x_i-x_j)^2.$$\nIf additionally $\\pi_{ij}>0$ for every ordered pair, then the sample\nexpression\n$$\\widehat V_{SYG}:=-\\frac12\\sum_{i,j\\in U}I_iI_j\\frac{\\Delta_{ij}}{\\pi_{ij}}(x_i-x_j)^2$$\nis unbiased for $\\operatorname{Var}_d(\\widehat T_{HT})$. If\n$\\Delta_{ij}\\le0$ for every distinct pair, this condition guarantees that\n$\\widehat V_{SYG}$ is nonnegative for every supported sample. It is a\nsufficient sign condition for the sample expression; the true variance in\nthe identity is nonnegative for every valid design.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The six weights sum to $2(2/5)+4(1/20)=1$ and every sample has size two; each unit's incident weights sum to $1/2$, so $\\pi_i=1/2$. Pair probabilities are $\\pi_{12}=\\pi_{34}=2/5$ and $1/20$ for cross pairs; by [F2], $\\Delta_{12}=\\Delta_{34}=2/5-1/4=3/20$ and $\\Delta_{ij}=1/20-1/4=-1/5$ for cross pairs. All $\\pi_{ij}$ are positive.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "For $y^A=(0,0,1,1)$, $x=(0,0,2,2)$. Samples $\\{1,2\\}$ and $\\{3,4\\}$ give SYG estimate zero; each cross sample gives $-\\Delta_{ij}(x_i-x_j)^2/\\pi_{ij}=16$. The six estimates are $0,0,16,16,16,16$ and have expectation $4(1/20)(16)=16/5$. HT totals are $0,4,2$ with probabilities $2/5,2/5,1/5$, so their mean is $2$ and variance is $(2/5)4+(2/5)4=16/5$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "For $y^B=(0,1,0,0)$, $x=(0,2,0,0)$. The HT total is $2$ or $0$ with probability $1/2$ each, so its variance is $1$. Sample $\\{1,2\\}$ has probability $2/5$ and SYG estimate $-\\Delta_{12}(x_1-x_2)^2/\\pi_{12}=-(3/20)(4)/(2/5)=-3/2$; samples $\\{2,3\\}$ and $\\{2,4\\}$ have estimate $16$ and the other three have zero. The expectation is $(2/5)(-3/2)+2(1/20)(16)=1$, equal to the variance despite the negative sample value; thus the nonpositive-Delta condition is sufficient but not necessary for all supported estimates to be nonnegative for a particular fixed population, as schedule $y^A$ in step 2.1 shows.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
